[Desktop Entry]
Name=Symmetria File Manager
Comment=Keyboard-first file manager, Electron build
# The daemon (symmetria-fm-electron.service) is resident, so this launches the
# COMMAND-LINE TOOL rather than the binary. Launching the binary would start a
# second process that finds the socket already claimed and exits — the whole
# point of a resident daemon is that opening a folder reaches the one already
# running, in the window the user already has their tabs in.
# By NAME, not by absolute path, and that is forced rather than chosen: a
# desktop entry has no home specifier — the field codes are %f %F %u %U %d %D
# %n %N %i %c %k %v %m and nothing else — so `%h/.local/bin/...` would be a
# literal, and a hardcoded /home/<user> path is worse. It therefore depends on
# the launcher's PATH including ~/.local/bin. `TryExec` is what makes that
# visible: a launcher hides the entry when it cannot resolve, instead of
# offering something that fails silently on click. The install script warns
# when the directory is off PATH.
TryExec=symmetria-fm-electron-cli
# %f and not %u: the tool takes a plain absolute path and would pass a file://
# URI through verbatim.
Exec=symmetria-fm-electron-cli open %f
# DEFERRED, deliberately, and said here so it does not read as an oversight:
# no asset with this name exists yet, so a launcher falls back to a generic
# icon. The Qt build's `assets/symmetria-fm.png` is the obvious source when
# somebody wants one, but sharing its NAME would make a launcher show one
# entry for two applications that are deliberately distinct.
Icon=symmetria-fm-electron
Type=Application
Categories=System;FileTools;FileManager;
MimeType=inode/directory;
Terminal=false
StartupNotify=false
# ── The identifier contract, which has three corners and two that matter ──
#
# This filename, the `desktopName` field in app/package.json, and the string
# below must all be `symmetria-fm-electron`. A unit test asserts all three,
# because they fail differently and two of them fail quietly:
#
#   * The BASENAME is what the portal resolves app info by. Drift there breaks
#     registration with an error only the journal sees.
#   * `desktopName` is what Electron hands Chromium, and Chromium sets the
#     Wayland `app_id` from it. THAT is what a Hyprland windowrule matches — so
#     renaming it silently moves the window off its workspace.
#   * StartupWMClass is a launcher and XWayland hint and drives NOTHING here.
#     Editing it to fix a window rule will do nothing at all. It is kept in step
#     anyway so the three never disagree.
#
# Deliberately NOT `symmetria-fm`, which belongs to the Qt build. The two run
# side by side until the rewrite reaches parity and the operator uses the Qt one
# every day; one identifier for both would make either compositor rule match the
# wrong window.
StartupWMClass=symmetria-fm-electron
